for t in ['Distinguish password verification, a reusable session, and authorization rules.', 'Use Swagger to send JSON requests and read actual HTTP responses.', 'Verify authenticated CRUD for games and scores and reject anonymous requests.', 'Explain password hashing, token expiry, and the limits of authentication using your logs.']: D.add_paragraph(t,style='List Bullet')
h('What you need')
p('Have the supplied IFT_Level2-Authentication project, Node.js and npm compatible with its dependencies, a code editor, Chrome or another browser, a working MongoDB database, and Word or another DOCX editor. Check node --version and npm --version before installing dependencies. Use the instructor-provided database setup if Atlas is not already configured.')
h('How this assignment works')
p('This is a configuration, testing, and explanation assignment. The authentication implementation is already provided. Complete the tasks through Swagger; change only .env or config.env unless the instructor explicitly assigns a coding extension. Use your own isolated coursework database and test records.')
p('The original Level 1 handout and some startup messages describe anonymous access. For this Level 2 exercise, a game or score request without a valid token must return 401. Do not remove authentication to reproduce the earlier behavior. Roles and ownership restrictions are not implemented in this build.')
h('Your submission')
p('Prepare one Word report with numbered screenshots, a completed results matrix, and a 90 to 120 word Security Log Analysis. Include the timestamped application log and package both files in a ZIP. Use the due date and submission location provided by your instructor in Canvas.')
page('1 Set up and start the project')
step(1,'Open IFT_Level2-Authentication in your editor. Open a terminal in the folder containing package.json and server.js. Run npm install and resolve any installation errors before continuing.')
step(2,'Copy config.env.example to .env only if .env does not already exist. Add your own settings. Do not overwrite a working configuration. The application loads .env first; config.env supplies values that are not already set.')
code('NODE_ENV=development\nPORT=4001\nAPI_VERSION=/api/v1\nDATABASE=mongodb://127.0.0.1:27017/ift458_level2\nSTUDENT_NAME=Your Full Name\nSTUDENT_ID=Your Student ID')
p('The local example requires MongoDB to be running on your computer. For Atlas, replace DATABASE_URL with the template below and fill the variables using your own database credentials. Replace <your-cluster> with your actual cluster host.')
code('DATABASE_URL=mongodb+srv://<db_username>:<db_password>@<your-cluster>/<db_name>\nDATABASE_USER=your_database_user\nDATABASE_PWD=your_database_password\nDATABASE_NAME=ift458_level2')
p('Use the exact variable names shown for this build. An Atlas database user is different from an application user registered through /users/signup. Never place real database credentials in screenshots or your submitted report.')
step(3,'If the instructor requires sample records, run npm run seed once in your isolated coursework database. This command deletes existing users and games before importing sample data. Skip seeding if you need to preserve those records; this assignment lets you create your own through the API.')
step(4,'Run npm test in the project terminal. Record the pass and fail totals. The tests use a temporary MongoDB database; they do not replace the manual evidence required below.')
step(5,'Run npm start. Keep this terminal open. Confirm that the server connects to MongoDB and that the startup log identifies you. Open http://localhost:4001/api-docs. If PORT was changed, use that port consistently.')
step(6,'Confirm the Swagger title includes Level 2 Resource Authentication. Restart the server after configuration or code changes. Avoid using an older deployed Level 1 URL for this assignment.')
h('Evidence 1')
p('Capture the startup success and your student identity without revealing credentials. Capture the automated test summary separately. If setup fails, record the exact error, command, and troubleshooting steps rather than claiming success.')
page('2 Register and obtain a session')
step(1,'In Swagger, expand POST /users/signup. Select Try it out, enter a unique coursework email and the JSON below, and select Execute. Read Server response. Expect 201 Created. Signup does not issue a token.')
code('{\n  "name": "Student One",\n  "email": "student.one@ift458.test",\n  "password": "Coursework12345",\n  "passwordConfirmation": "Coursework12345"\n}')
step(2,'Repeat signup with the same email. Expect 409 Conflict. Try a different email with mismatched passwordConfirmation. Expect 400. Do not change stored database records to make these tests pass.')
step(3,'Expand POST /users/login and submit the registered email and password. Expect 200 with token, expiresAt, and a public user profile. The token is valid for 24 hours. Capture the response structure, but cover the complete token in submitted screenshots.')
code('{\n  "email": "student.one@ift458.test",\n  "password": "Coursework12345"\n}')
step(4,'Test a wrong password and an unregistered email separately. Expect 401 for both and the same message, Incorrect email or password. Explain why the two failures do not disclose which emails exist.')
step(5,'Select Authorize at the top of Swagger. Paste only the token value into bearerAuth, choose Authorize, and close the dialog. Swagger adds the Bearer prefix automatically. For a separate HTTP client, use the header shown below.')
code('Authorization: Bearer <your token>')
h('Understand the credential')
p('The token is an opaque session credential, not a JWT to decode. The sessions collection stores its SHA-256 hash, user reference, and expiry. Passwords are stored as bcrypt hashes. The server checks token expiry and whether the associated user still exists on each protected request.')
p('Do not paste tokens into URLs, query parameters, logs, or your report. Swagger Logout removes the credential from Swagger requests; this build has no server logout route, so that action does not revoke a copied token.')
h('Evidence 2')
p('Capture signup success, duplicate or invalid signup, login success with token covered, and both invalid-login responses. With read-only database access, confirm the user password is a bcrypt hash rather than the submitted password. Never edit the hash.')
page('How HTTP carries authentication')
h('Stateless HTTP and application sessions')
p('HTTP does not automatically remember that a previous request logged in. Each protected request must supply a credential. An application can maintain sessions on top of HTTP. In this project, MongoDB holds session records, so the application uses server-side state even though HTTP is stateless. A persistent network connection does not replace the credential check.')
h('What goes into the request')
p('The first login request carries email and password in a JSON body. After successful verification, the server returns an opaque token. For later game and score operations, the client sends that token in the Authorization header instead of resending the password. In Swagger, paste only the token into Authorize; Swagger constructs the header.')
code('GET /api/v1/games HTTP/1.1\nHost: localhost:4001\nAuthorization: Bearer <your-token>\nAccept: application/json')
p('For this application, the authentication middleware hashes the submitted token, finds an unexpired session, checks that the user exists, and allows the resource handler to run. Without that proof, the response is 401. The token is a credential even though it is not the user password.')
h('Why the header is useful and what makes transport safe')
p('The Authorization header is the standard location for a bearer credential. It keeps the token out of the URL, where it can leak through history and URL logs. The header itself is not encrypted. HTTPS encrypts HTTP headers and bodies in transit; ordinary HTTP does not. The localhost HTTP address is for local lab use. Network-accessible or deployed use requires HTTPS.')
p('A bearer token can be used by anyone who obtains it until it expires or is invalidated. The client developer tools, application server, and TLS-terminating proxies can see it. Do not share it, log the Authorization header, or expose it in screenshots. Clearing Swagger state does not revoke a token copied elsewhere.')
h('Check your understanding')
p('Explain why a successful login followed by a request without a token returns 401. Then explain why moving a password or token into a header does not make a plain HTTP connection secure. Finally, identify the server-side state used by this project.')
h('Further reading')
p('MDN Web Docs. Overview of HTTP, section HTTP is stateless but not sessionless. https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Guides/Overview')
p('RFC 6750. The OAuth 2.0 Authorization Framework Bearer Token Usage, sections 2.1 and 5. https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6750')
p('These readings explain HTTP and the bearer convention. This lab does not implement a complete OAuth authorization flow.')
page('3 Demonstrate authenticated resource operations')
p('Keep Swagger authorized. Create test records bearing your name so you can identify them later. Copy each returned _id before editing or deleting a record. All paths below are relative to /api/v1.')
h('Games')
code('{\n  "title": "Student One Test Game",\n  "developer": "Course Lab",\n  "description": "Temporary record for authentication testing",\n  "platform": "pc",\n  "submittedBy": "Student One"\n}')
p('POST /games with this body, then GET /games and GET /games/{id}. PATCH your record with {"description":"Updated during the lab"}. Confirm the change with another GET. Keep the record until you finish the anonymous tests on the next page.')
h('Scores')
code('{\n  "gameTitle": "Student One Test Game",\n  "points": 1200,\n  "playerEmail": "student.one@ift458.test",\n  "platform": "pc"\n}')
p('POST /scores three times, using different points values. Expect status submitted for each new score. GET /scores and locate your three records; other records may also appear. GET one score by its _id and PATCH it with {"points":1300}. Confirm the stored change.')
